00:04:56.950 --> 00:05:00.250
So supply chains, from the
back office to the front page.

00:05:00.250 --> 00:05:02.110
The COVID-19
pandemic, it's really

00:05:02.110 --> 00:05:05.200
caused unprecedented disruption
to global supply chains.

00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:06.700
Manufacturing
shutdowns, as well as

00:05:06.700 --> 00:05:08.800
labor shortages and
a dramatic shift

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:10.900
in consumer spending
from services to goods,

00:05:10.900 --> 00:05:13.570
have caused massive disruptions
to the previously invisible

00:05:13.570 --> 00:05:15.097
flow of materials.

00:05:15.097 --> 00:05:17.680
This disruption can be seen in
the container ships waiting off

00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:19.400
the coast of
Southern California,

00:05:19.400 --> 00:05:22.060
waiting to dock in Los
Angeles and Long Beach.

00:05:22.060 --> 00:05:24.550
It can be seen in the
reconsideration of reshoring

00:05:24.550 --> 00:05:27.730
American manufacturing capacity
or the delays in building

00:05:27.730 --> 00:05:29.855
and repairing homes due
to supply shortages.

00:05:29.855 --> 00:05:32.230
It can be seen in the inflation
that you see at the store

00:05:32.230 --> 00:05:34.300
shelves and at the gas pump.

00:05:34.300 --> 00:05:36.790
And even Santa Claus
can't fix these problems,

00:05:36.790 --> 00:05:39.250
and he has the most
advanced logistics operation

00:05:39.250 --> 00:05:42.408
known to mankind, or elfkind.

00:05:42.408 --> 00:05:44.200
Instead, the task of
ensuring supply chains

00:05:44.200 --> 00:05:45.790
continue to function
so we literally

00:05:45.790 --> 00:05:48.210
have food on our plate
and fuel in our cars, that

00:05:48.210 --> 00:05:50.710
falls to the legions of hard
working supply chain management

00:05:50.710 --> 00:05:51.310
professionals.

00:05:51.310 --> 00:05:53.230
And as a data
scientist, I'm really

00:05:53.230 --> 00:05:56.020
quite proud to be able to use
my quantitative technical skills

00:05:56.020 --> 00:05:58.653
to help these efforts.

00:05:58.653 --> 00:06:00.070
So you may be
asking yourself, how

00:06:00.070 --> 00:06:02.320
can analytics be used in the
management of supply chains.

00:06:02.320 --> 00:06:03.850
Depending upon your
background, this slide

00:06:03.850 --> 00:06:05.200
may be old news
to you, but I find

00:06:05.200 --> 00:06:06.490
it's useful to
discuss supply chain

00:06:06.490 --> 00:06:08.870
analytics in the framework
of descriptive, predictive,

00:06:08.870 --> 00:06:10.313
and prescriptive analytics.

00:06:10.313 --> 00:06:12.730
Now, descriptive analytics,
the biggest and most important

00:06:12.730 --> 00:06:14.855
part of the pyramid, includes
business intelligence

00:06:14.855 --> 00:06:17.537
and diagnostic analytics
of the supply chain.

00:06:17.537 --> 00:06:19.120
How many of my trucks
arrived on time?

00:06:19.120 --> 00:06:20.920
How productive were my
manufacturing plants

00:06:20.920 --> 00:06:22.030
and my warehouses?

00:06:22.030 --> 00:06:25.230
How are these metrics trending,
and are any of them worrisome?

00:06:25.230 --> 00:06:26.520
Predictive analytics is next.

00:06:26.520 --> 00:06:27.750
How do we predict
what's going to happen

00:06:27.750 --> 00:06:28.792
next in our supply chain?

00:06:28.792 --> 00:06:30.960
That includes things
like sales forecasting,

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:33.503
as that will be discussed
next, as well as forecasting

00:06:33.503 --> 00:06:34.920
costs for your
supply chain inputs

00:06:34.920 --> 00:06:37.105
and some types of
supply chain simulation.

00:06:37.105 --> 00:06:38.730
Finally, the top of
the pyramid, which,

00:06:38.730 --> 00:06:41.100
by volume, should occupy
the least amount of time

00:06:41.100 --> 00:06:42.540
is prescriptive analytics.

00:06:42.540 --> 00:06:44.620
What should I do
with my supply chain?

00:06:44.620 --> 00:06:46.200
This is my wheelhouse
and includes

00:06:46.200 --> 00:06:48.840
things like supply chain
network design, discussed today,

00:06:48.840 --> 00:06:51.180
as well as inventory
optimization and other types

00:06:51.180 --> 00:06:53.780
of supply chain simulation.

00:06:53.780 --> 00:06:55.800
Now, a key point
for this audience,

00:06:55.800 --> 00:06:58.150
especially, is that open
source technologies are really

00:06:58.150 --> 00:07:00.650
changing the landscape in supply
chain management and supply

00:07:00.650 --> 00:07:02.057
chain analytics.

00:07:02.057 --> 00:07:03.890
When you look at each
of these capabilities,

00:07:03.890 --> 00:07:06.650
you can mentally pick out
which R or Python packages

00:07:06.650 --> 00:07:08.188
you could use for them.

00:07:08.188 --> 00:07:10.730
This really is a sea change from
when I started in the supply

00:07:10.730 --> 00:07:13.280
chain analytics field
about a decade ago,

00:07:13.280 --> 00:07:16.730
when the toolkit consisted
of Excel for data analysis,

00:07:16.730 --> 00:07:19.580
SPSS for statistics, and
commercial visualization

00:07:19.580 --> 00:07:22.730
software like Tableau.

00:07:22.730 --> 00:07:25.340
So what is supply
chain network design?

00:07:25.340 --> 00:07:27.380
Put plainly, it's the
strategic analysis

00:07:27.380 --> 00:07:29.780
of the locations and activities
of the various elements

00:07:29.780 --> 00:07:31.410
of the firm's supply chain.

00:07:31.410 --> 00:07:34.070
For example, if
I'm a manufacturer,

00:07:34.070 --> 00:07:36.680
where should I locate
warehouses for my raw materials,

00:07:36.680 --> 00:07:39.110
the stuff I use to
make other stuff,

00:07:39.110 --> 00:07:40.640
or my semi-finished
goods, which are

00:07:40.640 --> 00:07:42.848
items that are only partway
through the manufacturing

00:07:42.848 --> 00:07:43.962
process?

00:07:43.962 --> 00:07:45.920
Where should I put my
manufacturing facilities,

00:07:45.920 --> 00:07:46.430
my plants?

00:07:46.430 --> 00:07:47.908
Where should they go?

00:07:47.908 --> 00:07:49.700
Where should I put my
distribution centers,

00:07:49.700 --> 00:07:51.102
my warehouses for
finished goods?

00:07:51.102 --> 00:07:52.310
Where should they be located?

00:07:55.500 --> 00:07:57.200
And if I'm a
retailer, how should

00:07:57.200 --> 00:07:59.780
I use my stores as a
link in my supply chain?

00:07:59.780 --> 00:08:01.860
For example, many
leading retailers,

00:08:01.860 --> 00:08:03.740
including my former
employer Target,

00:08:03.740 --> 00:08:05.660
use their stores
as strategic assets

00:08:05.660 --> 00:08:07.100
for shipping e-commerce orders.

00:08:07.100 --> 00:08:08.900
Well, from where
should we do that?

00:08:08.900 --> 00:08:09.950
That's a big question.

00:08:09.950 --> 00:08:12.740
That's a supply chain
network design question.

00:08:12.740 --> 00:08:14.167
Supply chain
network design seeks

00:08:14.167 --> 00:08:16.250
to answer these big
questions, all with the intent

00:08:16.250 --> 00:08:19.820
to understand the impacts on
profitability, cost, service,

00:08:19.820 --> 00:08:22.820
and other key metrics and with
a goal to minimize or maximize

00:08:22.820 --> 00:08:27.710
one or two key metrics,
usually cost or profitability.

00:08:27.710 --> 00:08:30.260
Today, I'll be focusing on
a relatively simple example

00:08:30.260 --> 00:08:33.350
of supply chain network design,
a distribution network with two

00:08:33.350 --> 00:08:36.830
echelons, the distribution
centers, or warehouses,

00:08:36.830 --> 00:08:39.804
and customers that are
serviced by those warehouses.

00:08:39.804 --> 00:08:41.179
In designing the
supply chain, we

00:08:41.179 --> 00:08:44.059
ask ourselves, given a customer
base of demand aggregated

00:08:44.059 --> 00:08:46.460
to a city level,
one, where should we

00:08:46.460 --> 00:08:49.310
open distribution centers
to satisfy that demand,

00:08:49.310 --> 00:08:52.970
and two, what customers should
be serviced by, or aligned to,

00:08:52.970 --> 00:08:55.250
each distribution center?

00:08:55.250 --> 00:08:57.780
While most real life supply
chain design problems are much,

00:08:57.780 --> 00:08:59.810
much more complex
than this example,

00:08:59.810 --> 00:09:02.330
I hope it will be informative
on how practitioners approach

00:09:02.330 --> 00:09:04.580
these kinds of problems and
how we can use open source

00:09:04.580 --> 00:09:06.650
technology for them.

00:09:06.650 --> 00:09:08.563
So how do we do this?

00:09:08.563 --> 00:09:09.980
How can we answer
these questions?

00:09:09.980 --> 00:09:12.280
We can answer them with
optimization modeling.

00:09:12.280 --> 00:09:14.650
Optimization modeling is
a mathematical technique

00:09:14.650 --> 00:09:16.300
that allows a
practitioner to optimize

00:09:16.300 --> 00:09:18.640
a set of interconnected
decisions, where

00:09:18.640 --> 00:09:21.910
the decisions are constrained
by real world limitations.

00:09:21.910 --> 00:09:24.340
Optimization solvers take
the decision options,

00:09:24.340 --> 00:09:26.350
formulate it as a
mathematical model,

00:09:26.350 --> 00:09:29.260
and return the set of decisions
that optimizes, minimizes

00:09:29.260 --> 00:09:32.380
or maximizes, an equation
that includes the decisions

00:09:32.380 --> 00:09:33.340
as inputs.

00:09:33.340 --> 00:09:36.347
This equation is formally
known as an objective function.

00:09:36.347 --> 00:09:38.680
If anyone on here has an
operations research background,

00:09:38.680 --> 00:09:41.360
I realize it's not the precise
mathematical definition,

00:09:41.360 --> 00:09:44.330
but I find it to be useful
in explaining the field.

00:09:44.330 --> 00:09:47.020
So in our case, the decisions
we need to make are one,

00:09:47.020 --> 00:09:48.580
how many distribution
centers to open

00:09:48.580 --> 00:09:51.460
and where should we locate them
and two, what customers rely

00:09:51.460 --> 00:09:53.845
on each distribution center?

00:09:53.845 --> 00:09:55.220
You can see that
these decisions,

00:09:55.220 --> 00:09:57.580
they're interconnected because
I can't align a customer

00:09:57.580 --> 00:09:59.330
to a distribution
center in a certain city

00:09:59.330 --> 00:10:01.220
unless I also open
a distribution

00:10:01.220 --> 00:10:03.610
center in that city.

00:10:03.610 --> 00:10:06.550
And the limitations on our
decisions, or our constraints,

00:10:06.550 --> 00:10:09.580
they're, one, that all
customer demand must be met.

00:10:09.580 --> 00:10:13.510
We may have a minimum or maximum
number of distribution centers.

00:10:13.510 --> 00:10:17.470
We may really only realistically
open distribution centers

00:10:17.470 --> 00:10:18.530
in certain locations.

00:10:18.530 --> 00:10:21.780
So we have to start with
a candidate set of cities.

00:10:21.780 --> 00:10:24.960
We may have a maximum volume
per distribution center.

00:10:24.960 --> 00:10:27.120
It's also called the capacity.

00:10:27.120 --> 00:10:30.390
And for certain reasons, we
may put a maximum distance

00:10:30.390 --> 00:10:32.880
between a customer and its
servicing distribution center,

00:10:32.880 --> 00:10:35.397
and we'll see that
later in an example.

00:10:35.397 --> 00:10:37.230
So we'll shortly
transition to the shiny app

00:10:37.230 --> 00:10:38.897
I built for the
webinar, but first, I'll

00:10:38.897 --> 00:10:39.930
introduce the scenario.

00:10:39.930 --> 00:10:41.733
Now, because this is
a simplified scenario,

00:10:41.733 --> 00:10:43.650
this is a simplified
version of an app I would

00:10:43.650 --> 00:10:44.865
use for a regular project.

00:10:47.500 --> 00:10:49.960
You are supply chain data
scientist at Heartland Widgets,

00:10:49.960 --> 00:10:51.855
Incorporated.

00:10:51.855 --> 00:10:53.230
Your company is
the market leader

00:10:53.230 --> 00:10:55.240
in widget sales across
an eight-state area

00:10:55.240 --> 00:10:56.590
of the American Midwest.

00:10:56.590 --> 00:10:58.720
The pandemic-induced
shift in consumer demand

00:10:58.720 --> 00:11:01.978
from services to goods, as
well as stay at home orders,

00:11:01.978 --> 00:11:04.270
has led to a boom in demand
for your company's flagship

00:11:04.270 --> 00:11:06.810
product, the at-home widget.

00:11:06.810 --> 00:11:08.430
Faced with record
demand, the director

00:11:08.430 --> 00:11:10.805
of supply chain network planning
has asked you to, quote,

00:11:10.805 --> 00:11:13.970
reimagine the company's
distribution network.

00:11:13.970 --> 00:11:17.228
You are to recommend where to
open Heartland's Distribution

00:11:17.228 --> 00:11:19.520
Centers, as well as the
customer-to-distribution-center

00:11:19.520 --> 00:11:23.950
alignment to achieve
a minimal cost.

00:11:23.950 --> 00:11:27.770
Heartland Widgets has customers
in 117 Midwestern cities,

00:11:27.770 --> 00:11:29.770
spread from the Dakotas
in the West, all the way

00:11:29.770 --> 00:11:31.990
to my home state of
Indiana and East.

00:11:31.990 --> 00:11:34.400
Each city's demand is
proportional to its population.

00:11:34.400 --> 00:11:37.150
So for example, Chicago,
Illinois, which is a big city,

00:11:37.150 --> 00:11:38.800
may have heard of
it, has more demand

00:11:38.800 --> 00:11:39.842
than Fargo, North Dakota.

00:11:42.770 --> 00:11:44.330
Heartland is
considering 20 cities

00:11:44.330 --> 00:11:46.520
for potential
distribution center sites.

00:11:46.520 --> 00:11:50.210
Because each distribution center
will cost $1 million to open,

00:11:50.210 --> 00:11:52.640
it may not be cost effective
to open distribution centers

00:11:52.640 --> 00:11:53.630
in all 20 cities.

00:11:53.630 --> 00:11:57.060
But we're keeping
our options open.

00:11:57.060 --> 00:11:59.365
So let's get a little
bit more technical here.

00:11:59.365 --> 00:12:01.490
The decisions we need to
make for Heartland Widgets

00:12:01.490 --> 00:12:03.140
can be represented by variables.

00:12:03.140 --> 00:12:04.910
When we ask what
customer should we

00:12:04.910 --> 00:12:06.920
align to which
distribution center,

00:12:06.920 --> 00:12:09.800
this is equivalent to asking a
set of yes, no decisions that

00:12:09.800 --> 00:12:11.570
must be made in concert.

00:12:11.570 --> 00:12:13.340
For each customer
city I, should I

00:12:13.340 --> 00:12:17.570
service it by a distribution
center in city J, yes or no?

00:12:17.570 --> 00:12:19.460
In making our
decisions, we're limited

00:12:19.460 --> 00:12:22.580
to having exactly one
yes per customer city.

00:12:22.580 --> 00:12:26.330
It has to be serviced by
exactly one distribution center.

00:12:26.330 --> 00:12:27.830
Relatedly, we need
to make decisions

00:12:27.830 --> 00:12:29.900
about how many distribution
centers to open

00:12:29.900 --> 00:12:31.550
and where to locate them.

00:12:31.550 --> 00:12:33.380
This is a series of
if, then decisions

00:12:33.380 --> 00:12:35.450
related to alignment decisions.

00:12:35.450 --> 00:12:37.880
If at least one customer is
aligned to a distribution

00:12:37.880 --> 00:12:40.670
center in, for example,
Omaha, Nebraska,

00:12:40.670 --> 00:12:44.588
then we must open a
distribution center in Omaha.

00:12:44.588 --> 00:12:46.130
The decision sets
are interconnected.

00:12:46.130 --> 00:12:48.170
We cannot separate our
decisions on where to locate

00:12:48.170 --> 00:12:50.660
our distribution centers from
our decisions on how to align

00:12:50.660 --> 00:12:53.690
customers to
distribution centers.

00:12:53.690 --> 00:12:56.300
Mathematical optimization
and, more specifically,

00:12:56.300 --> 00:12:58.790
the techniques of linear
and integer programming

00:12:58.790 --> 00:13:03.750
are ideal for making these
kinds of interrelated decisions.

00:13:03.750 --> 00:13:05.513
When we use mathematical
optimization,

00:13:05.513 --> 00:13:06.930
including for
supply chain design,

00:13:06.930 --> 00:13:08.670
we're trying to
minimize or maximize

00:13:08.670 --> 00:13:11.423
some equation that is the
function of our decisions.

00:13:11.423 --> 00:13:12.840
In this example,
Heartland Widgets

00:13:12.840 --> 00:13:15.750
wants to design the supply chain
with the lowest total cost.

00:13:15.750 --> 00:13:17.490
The supply chain
costs in this example

00:13:17.490 --> 00:13:20.280
are, one, the opening cost for
distribution centers, which

00:13:20.280 --> 00:13:23.360
are priced at $1 million
each, two, the handling

00:13:23.360 --> 00:13:25.110
costs with the
distribution centers, which

00:13:25.110 --> 00:13:27.912
is one cent per
unit, per widget,

00:13:27.912 --> 00:13:30.120
and three, the transportation
costs from distribution

00:13:30.120 --> 00:13:33.090
centers to customers, which
are five cents per unit mile.

00:13:33.090 --> 00:13:36.670
Now, a unit mile is
moving one unit one mile.

00:13:36.670 --> 00:13:39.000
So for example, if you have
two widgets, two units,

00:13:39.000 --> 00:13:41.220
and you move them
100 miles, that's

00:13:41.220 --> 00:13:43.740
2 times 100, 200 unit miles.

00:13:43.740 --> 00:13:50.280
At $0.05 each, that's $10 to
move to two units 100 miles.

00:13:50.280 --> 00:13:52.080
You add up these
three cost components,

00:13:52.080 --> 00:13:53.872
and it leads to the
total supply chain cost

00:13:53.872 --> 00:13:55.590
that we're trying to minimize.

00:13:55.590 --> 00:13:57.600
Now, one of the tenets
of supply chain design

00:13:57.600 --> 00:14:01.050
is that these cost components
can be inversely related

00:14:01.050 --> 00:14:02.950
to each other.

00:14:02.950 --> 00:14:05.590
For example, the more
distribution centers you have,

00:14:05.590 --> 00:14:09.280
the closer you are on
average to your customers.

00:14:09.280 --> 00:14:11.743
And if you're closer to
your customers, on average,

00:14:11.743 --> 00:14:14.160
you're going to spend less on
getting the product to them,

00:14:14.160 --> 00:14:16.455
on that transportation cost,
because it's just closer.

00:14:19.200 --> 00:14:21.845
However, the more
distribution centers you have,

00:14:21.845 --> 00:14:23.970
the more you spend to open
them in the first place.

00:14:23.970 --> 00:14:25.280
So there's an
inverse relationship

00:14:25.280 --> 00:14:26.905
between the distribution
center opening

00:14:26.905 --> 00:14:29.240
cost and the transportation
cost to get the product

00:14:29.240 --> 00:14:30.272
to your customer.

00:14:30.272 --> 00:14:32.480
The more distribution centers
you have, the more cost

00:14:32.480 --> 00:14:35.270
to open them, and the less you
spend on the transportation,

00:14:35.270 --> 00:14:37.780
inverse relationship.

00:14:37.780 --> 00:14:41.470
Optimization modeling helps us
figure out these relationships

00:14:41.470 --> 00:14:43.960
and find the optimal solution
that takes into account

00:14:43.960 --> 00:14:47.410
the relationships
between the costs.

00:14:47.410 --> 00:14:49.390
All right, so because
this is R meetup

00:14:49.390 --> 00:14:51.160
and I'm going to
demo a Shiny app,

00:14:51.160 --> 00:14:53.320
I want to go over
the packages I used.

00:14:53.320 --> 00:14:55.690
This is a tidyverse-centric
solution for data prep,

00:14:55.690 --> 00:15:00.020
but I also use DT,
data table, and vroom.

00:15:00.020 --> 00:15:02.540
Shinydashboard is used
for visualization,

00:15:02.540 --> 00:15:05.540
and I use rhandsontable to
allow for interactive adjustment

00:15:05.540 --> 00:15:08.265
to input tables.

00:15:08.265 --> 00:15:09.900
Leaflet is used as
a mapping solution.

00:15:09.900 --> 00:15:11.970
And the core of the
optimization modeling

00:15:11.970 --> 00:15:15.570
rests with the ompr package
and the open source symphony

00:15:15.570 --> 00:15:18.930
optimization solver.

00:15:18.930 --> 00:15:22.470
As an aside, the COIN-OR
Foundation, the organization

00:15:22.470 --> 00:15:25.110
that maintains symphony and
other open source optimization

00:15:25.110 --> 00:15:28.080
tools like pulp and
piano for Python users,

00:15:28.080 --> 00:15:29.730
they need financial help.

00:15:29.730 --> 00:15:33.740
Please consider donating
via that GitHub link.

00:15:33.740 --> 00:15:34.240
All right.

00:15:34.240 --> 00:15:36.520
From here I'm going
to work from the app,

00:15:36.520 --> 00:15:39.670
but I have slides for backup in
case of technical difficulties.

00:15:39.670 --> 00:15:42.880
I host my app on Shinyapps.io.

00:15:42.880 --> 00:15:45.460
You'll see the address, but to
avoid having technical issues,

00:15:45.460 --> 00:15:50.960
I'd ask that nobody try to load
it while I'm giving the demo.

00:15:50.960 --> 00:15:51.460
All right.

00:15:51.460 --> 00:15:55.510
So this is a pretty
sparse app because it's

00:15:55.510 --> 00:15:57.400
a fairly simple example.

00:15:57.400 --> 00:16:01.900
And first step is
uploading our data.

00:16:01.900 --> 00:16:06.220
So we really only have two sets
of input data, our customer

00:16:06.220 --> 00:16:08.500
information and our
distribution center information.

00:16:08.500 --> 00:16:10.570
I've prepped the data.

00:16:10.570 --> 00:16:14.030
So we can you use room here.

00:16:14.030 --> 00:16:16.653
And these are small files,
but even if they were large,

00:16:16.653 --> 00:16:19.070
it'd still upload pretty quickly
because we're using room.

00:16:23.900 --> 00:16:26.900
And saying yes, it's
going to be used.

00:16:26.900 --> 00:16:28.010
All right.

00:16:28.010 --> 00:16:29.960
So now I'm going to
inspect this upload data

00:16:29.960 --> 00:16:31.880
and talk a little
bit through it.

00:16:31.880 --> 00:16:33.990
First our customer data.

00:16:33.990 --> 00:16:36.500
So our customers
are as mentioned

00:16:36.500 --> 00:16:40.960
117 Midwestern cities.

00:16:40.960 --> 00:16:45.370
And for each of these, we have
city names, state, latitude

00:16:45.370 --> 00:16:47.320
and longitude,
their demand, which

00:16:47.320 --> 00:16:50.030
is proportional to
their population.

00:16:50.030 --> 00:16:52.120
And then we have this
column called maximum miles

00:16:52.120 --> 00:16:53.320
to distribution center.

00:16:53.320 --> 00:16:57.130
You'll see later on in an
example how we would use this.

00:16:57.130 --> 00:16:59.642
Essentially if you want to
enforce a maximum distance

00:16:59.642 --> 00:17:01.600
from a customer to the
distribution center that

00:17:01.600 --> 00:17:06.040
services it, you would set this
column to that distance limit.

00:17:06.040 --> 00:17:07.839
Right now I don't
care about that,

00:17:07.839 --> 00:17:10.510
so I set this number so insanely
high that it doesn't really

00:17:10.510 --> 00:17:12.450
matter.

00:17:12.450 --> 00:17:14.310
Finally, we have this
column called Include,

00:17:14.310 --> 00:17:17.640
and this is a nice thing
about using our hands on table

00:17:17.640 --> 00:17:19.890
because you can actually
dynamically update

00:17:19.890 --> 00:17:22.800
the table that you use
later on in your app.

00:17:22.800 --> 00:17:25.319
So everything has
a checkmark here.

00:17:25.319 --> 00:17:27.660
If I uncheck any of
these, they would not

00:17:27.660 --> 00:17:30.918
be used in the
optimization model.

00:17:30.918 --> 00:17:32.710
So we're going to
display the customer map.

00:17:32.710 --> 00:17:35.530
You see these 117 cities
across the Midwest.

00:17:35.530 --> 00:17:39.770
And just as an example of
how R Hands on Table works,

00:17:39.770 --> 00:17:42.350
I wanted to get rid of
Rapid City, South Dakota.

00:17:42.350 --> 00:17:43.490
You see it's down here.

00:17:48.280 --> 00:17:49.190
Now it's gone.

00:17:49.190 --> 00:17:51.130
But I do care about
our Rapid City,

00:17:51.130 --> 00:17:52.400
so I'm going to keep it back.

00:17:52.400 --> 00:17:52.900
All right.

00:17:52.900 --> 00:17:55.790
So that's how we're using our
hands on table for the customer

00:17:55.790 --> 00:17:56.290
inputs.

00:17:59.140 --> 00:18:00.640
Distribution center similarly.

00:18:00.640 --> 00:18:06.040
We have 20 candidate cities
across the Midwest for--

00:18:06.040 --> 00:18:08.170
to put our distribution
centers in.

00:18:08.170 --> 00:18:09.910
We have a latitude
our longitude,

00:18:09.910 --> 00:18:12.160
our capacity, which
is the maximum volume

00:18:12.160 --> 00:18:14.290
that it can have a line to it.

00:18:14.290 --> 00:18:17.920
This is effectively infinite
compared to customer demand

00:18:17.920 --> 00:18:20.190
because it's a very,
very high number.

00:18:20.190 --> 00:18:22.920
Each of them costs
$1 million to open

00:18:22.920 --> 00:18:27.540
and our handling cost is $0.01
per unit as mentioned earlier.

00:18:27.540 --> 00:18:29.400
And this check mark allow--

00:18:29.400 --> 00:18:31.950
essentially says, do I want
to consider this location?

00:18:31.950 --> 00:18:34.590
If I uncheck any of
these, that location

00:18:34.590 --> 00:18:36.810
would just be taken out of
consideration right away.

00:18:40.930 --> 00:18:45.010
So finally we have
some inputs here

00:18:45.010 --> 00:18:47.110
before we run the optimization.

00:18:47.110 --> 00:18:49.720
So I mentioned earlier that
you can put a constraint

00:18:49.720 --> 00:18:53.450
around your minimum and maximum
number of distribution centers.

00:18:53.450 --> 00:18:56.790
And so I'm going to
start off by saying

00:18:56.790 --> 00:18:58.920
I'm going to have
a minimum of one,

00:18:58.920 --> 00:19:03.050
I need a warehouse somewhere,
and a maximum of 20.

00:19:03.050 --> 00:19:06.920
Because there's only 20 options,
here having 20 is your maximum.

00:19:06.920 --> 00:19:11.030
It's kind of like saying I'm
willing to take any of them.

00:19:11.030 --> 00:19:15.393
The transportation cost per
unit mile is $0.05 as mentioned,

00:19:15.393 --> 00:19:17.060
and these are some
more technical things

00:19:17.060 --> 00:19:17.870
I won't get into.

00:19:21.880 --> 00:19:22.870
All right.

00:19:22.870 --> 00:19:25.000
So when I click Run
optimization, what's

00:19:25.000 --> 00:19:28.360
happening now is my app
is taking the customer

00:19:28.360 --> 00:19:30.190
information, the
distribution center

00:19:30.190 --> 00:19:35.500
information, and the maximum and
minimum number of distributions

00:19:35.500 --> 00:19:37.840
centers and forming
an optimization

00:19:37.840 --> 00:19:41.020
model on the back side
on the Shiny app server.

00:19:41.020 --> 00:19:44.350
It then finds the optimal
solution the minimal cost

00:19:44.350 --> 00:19:45.730
and returns me--

00:19:45.730 --> 00:19:48.870
returns the outputs.

00:19:48.870 --> 00:19:50.670
Luckily it worked
as fast in this demo

00:19:50.670 --> 00:19:52.630
as it has in my practice.

00:19:52.630 --> 00:19:53.130
Right.

00:19:53.130 --> 00:19:56.250
So let's look and see what
those optimal solutions are.

00:19:56.250 --> 00:20:00.090
So to service all of
our customer demand

00:20:00.090 --> 00:20:03.780
for heartland widgets, the
optimal lowest cost solution

00:20:03.780 --> 00:20:05.550
is for distribution centers.

00:20:05.550 --> 00:20:09.450
One in Omaha, Nebraska, one here
in Minneapolis, one in Chicago,

00:20:09.450 --> 00:20:12.530
and one in my old
hometown in Indianapolis.

00:20:12.530 --> 00:20:17.620
You can see the cities that are
serviced by each distribution

00:20:17.620 --> 00:20:20.400
center are represented by their
color, you know, red for Omaha

00:20:20.400 --> 00:20:23.200
and blue for Indianapolis,
green for Chicago,

00:20:23.200 --> 00:20:24.970
yellow for Minneapolis.

00:20:24.970 --> 00:20:28.630
And they're geographically
separated as we would kind of

00:20:28.630 --> 00:20:30.970
expect because
you generally want

00:20:30.970 --> 00:20:35.050
to have your customers closer
to your distribution center.

00:20:35.050 --> 00:20:36.790
This table is an
alignment table,

00:20:36.790 --> 00:20:39.640
which is just the tabular
version of the map

00:20:39.640 --> 00:20:40.640
that we're seeing above.

00:20:40.640 --> 00:20:45.190
So for example Ames,
Iowa, which is over here,

00:20:45.190 --> 00:20:47.860
is serviced by the distribution
center in Omaha, Nebraska.

00:20:50.720 --> 00:20:53.860
The farthest distance from
a customer to its servicing

00:20:53.860 --> 00:20:56.570
and distribution
center is 415 miles,

00:20:56.570 --> 00:20:58.370
and that's from Rapid
City, South Dakota

00:20:58.370 --> 00:21:00.620
over here all the way to Omaha.

00:21:00.620 --> 00:21:03.110
Take note of that because
we'll actually come back

00:21:03.110 --> 00:21:05.100
to that in a little bit.

00:21:05.100 --> 00:21:08.270
So what is the
optimal cost here?

00:21:08.270 --> 00:21:12.350
The total supply chain cost that
can be the minimal total supply

00:21:12.350 --> 00:21:18.090
chain cost in this scenario
is $7.5 million over here.

00:21:18.090 --> 00:21:20.790
That includes $4 million
in distribution center

00:21:20.790 --> 00:21:23.220
opening cost, $1 million
per distribution center

00:21:23.220 --> 00:21:27.570
and four of them, $13,000 for
handling at the distribution

00:21:27.570 --> 00:21:32.410
centers, and about $3.5
million in transportation cost.

00:21:32.410 --> 00:21:37.110
So our total 4 million plus
13,000 plus 3.5 million

00:21:37.110 --> 00:21:40.635
equals $7.5 million, and that is
our absolute lowest total cost.

00:21:43.418 --> 00:21:45.460
Now I'm going to make a
small detour before we're

00:21:45.460 --> 00:21:47.440
going to do another
scenario to explain

00:21:47.440 --> 00:21:51.040
more what we mean by constraints
in supply chain network design.

00:21:51.040 --> 00:21:53.350
In optimization
modeling, constraints

00:21:53.350 --> 00:21:55.780
are mathematical
limitations on what set

00:21:55.780 --> 00:21:57.550
of decisions you can make.

00:21:57.550 --> 00:22:00.680
They correspond real
life limitations.

00:22:00.680 --> 00:22:03.530
The more constraints you have
in your model and in real life,

00:22:03.530 --> 00:22:05.510
the worse your objective
function becomes.

00:22:05.510 --> 00:22:09.200
So in this case, the more the
supply chain cost would be.

00:22:09.200 --> 00:22:11.900
Optimization modeling including
supply chain network design

00:22:11.900 --> 00:22:14.570
is an exercise in understanding
the trade offs involved

00:22:14.570 --> 00:22:17.600
and making constraints more or
less restrictive for tighter

00:22:17.600 --> 00:22:20.713
or looser, which
is the term of R.

00:22:20.713 --> 00:22:22.380
In designing a supply
chain, the tighter

00:22:22.380 --> 00:22:25.080
the constraints, the higher
the total cost becomes.

00:22:25.080 --> 00:22:27.120
Supply chain design
is partly in around

00:22:27.120 --> 00:22:29.580
understanding the additional
expenses associated

00:22:29.580 --> 00:22:32.077
with tighter constraints.

00:22:32.077 --> 00:22:34.410
Next we'll talk about what a
couple of those constraints

00:22:34.410 --> 00:22:35.890
may look like.

00:22:35.890 --> 00:22:39.330
So first we see that the
lowest total supply chain cost

00:22:39.330 --> 00:22:41.940
occurs when we have four
distribution centers

00:22:41.940 --> 00:22:43.620
in these cities up here.

00:22:47.670 --> 00:22:49.680
So what would happen
if we actually

00:22:49.680 --> 00:22:54.185
force our model to have exactly
three distribution centers?

00:22:54.185 --> 00:22:56.310
How will that change our
cost, how will that change

00:22:56.310 --> 00:22:57.930
what our network looks like?

00:22:57.930 --> 00:23:00.930
This is actually quite
straightforward to see.

00:23:00.930 --> 00:23:04.530
All we need to do is go
back to minimum and maximum

00:23:04.530 --> 00:23:07.750
number of distribution
center constraints.

00:23:07.750 --> 00:23:11.250
So I'm going to change
the minimum to three

00:23:11.250 --> 00:23:12.750
and the maximum to three.

00:23:12.750 --> 00:23:14.130
Because these are
integer values,

00:23:14.130 --> 00:23:16.770
by forcing your minimum and
maximum to both be three,

00:23:16.770 --> 00:23:20.070
you're going to end up with
three distribution centers.

00:23:20.070 --> 00:23:21.450
And click Run optimization.

00:23:24.532 --> 00:23:25.990
And this optimization
model is just

00:23:25.990 --> 00:23:28.323
running in the background
with the additional constraint

00:23:28.323 --> 00:23:32.560
that it needs exactly
three distribution centers.

00:23:32.560 --> 00:23:35.140
I'm going to go back to
my results, it'll refresh.

00:23:35.140 --> 00:23:38.890
And now we see that instead
of four distribution centers,

00:23:38.890 --> 00:23:41.080
we have the required three.

00:23:41.080 --> 00:23:45.310
And it's the same way out
except now Indianapolis is gone.

00:23:45.310 --> 00:23:47.530
Sorry to my Hoosier friends.

00:23:47.530 --> 00:23:53.890
And so question then, what does
this do to our overall cost?

00:23:53.890 --> 00:23:55.990
Because as I mentioned,
the tighter you

00:23:55.990 --> 00:23:58.390
make your constraints, the
worse your objective function

00:23:58.390 --> 00:24:00.460
becomes in this,
meaning the more

00:24:00.460 --> 00:24:04.090
you're going to pay in total.

00:24:04.090 --> 00:24:06.270
So let's look at these outputs.

00:24:06.270 --> 00:24:09.772
Our DC opening costs
are now $3 million

00:24:09.772 --> 00:24:11.730
because we only have
three distribution centers

00:24:11.730 --> 00:24:14.170
and they are $1 million apiece.

00:24:14.170 --> 00:24:16.168
Our handling cost is
the same because it's

00:24:16.168 --> 00:24:17.710
the same for all
distribution centers

00:24:17.710 --> 00:24:19.660
and we have the same
amount of volume.

00:24:19.660 --> 00:24:22.720
But our transportation
cost, which was $3.5 million

00:24:22.720 --> 00:24:26.156
before is now $4.6 million.

00:24:26.156 --> 00:24:30.230
So while our distribution
center opening costs went down,

00:24:30.230 --> 00:24:36.080
$1 million, our transportation
costs went up $1,100,000.

00:24:36.080 --> 00:24:42.180
And so our total cost went from
7.5 million to 7.6 million.

00:24:42.180 --> 00:24:45.210
So we see that this one
change increases the cost.

00:24:45.210 --> 00:24:49.680
Now, 100,000 is a lot of
money to a normal person.

00:24:49.680 --> 00:24:52.320
In the consideration of
a base of 7.5 million,

00:24:52.320 --> 00:24:55.810
it's not a big difference,
but it is an increase.

00:24:55.810 --> 00:24:59.220
So that's what we see
when we just force exactly

00:24:59.220 --> 00:25:01.900
three distribution centers.

00:25:01.900 --> 00:25:04.950
Next we'll see what happens when
we enforce a maximum distance

00:25:04.950 --> 00:25:07.200
constraint between our
distribution centers

00:25:07.200 --> 00:25:08.520
and our customers.

00:25:08.520 --> 00:25:11.250
This is often the case
with food supply chains

00:25:11.250 --> 00:25:13.770
because food is
perishable and you

00:25:13.770 --> 00:25:16.470
don't want your food outside of
a climate controlled warehouse

00:25:16.470 --> 00:25:18.990
for too long before
it gets to a store.

00:25:18.990 --> 00:25:21.390
And so oftentimes when
designing food supply chains,

00:25:21.390 --> 00:25:25.050
you would put in a
distance constraint.

00:25:25.050 --> 00:25:28.140
This is actually pretty easy
to do with our Anton table

00:25:28.140 --> 00:25:31.200
and using this maximum
miles to DC column.

00:25:31.200 --> 00:25:40.060
I'm going to say a maximum
of 400 miles between all

00:25:40.060 --> 00:25:42.190
of my customers and my
distribution centers.

00:25:42.190 --> 00:25:43.810
As you may remember,
the distance

00:25:43.810 --> 00:25:47.230
between Rapid City, South Dakota
and the distribution center

00:25:47.230 --> 00:25:51.340
in Omaha, Nebraska
was 415 miles.

00:25:51.340 --> 00:25:53.740
So by adding this
additional constraint,

00:25:53.740 --> 00:25:55.700
that alignment is not possible.

00:25:55.700 --> 00:25:58.590
It's not going to be allowed.

00:25:58.590 --> 00:25:59.840
So we just change that column.

00:26:04.350 --> 00:26:08.685
Change it back to 1 in
20 and run optimization.

00:26:16.620 --> 00:26:17.932
I give this a few more seconds.

00:26:17.932 --> 00:26:19.890
And if it doesn't work
as quickly as I thought,

00:26:19.890 --> 00:26:21.140
I'll just go on my slide show.

00:26:24.090 --> 00:26:24.590
OK.

00:26:29.038 --> 00:26:31.580
Instead of weighing that, we'll
just go back to the slideshow

00:26:31.580 --> 00:26:32.080
here.

00:26:32.080 --> 00:26:38.340
So what would happen if we did
force a 400 mile constraint?

00:26:38.340 --> 00:26:42.870
Instead of-- we still have
four distribution centers

00:26:42.870 --> 00:26:44.730
as the optimal setting.

00:26:44.730 --> 00:26:47.670
But instead of a warehouse
in Omaha, Nebraska,

00:26:47.670 --> 00:26:50.230
we would have it in Sioux
Falls, South Dakota.

00:26:50.230 --> 00:26:50.730
Why?

00:26:50.730 --> 00:26:54.090
Because that is the
distribution center

00:26:54.090 --> 00:26:57.720
that allows Sioux Falls, which
is a-- sorry, Rapid City, South

00:26:57.720 --> 00:27:01.020
Dakota over here, which
is a very remote customer

00:27:01.020 --> 00:27:03.480
to be within 400 miles.

00:27:03.480 --> 00:27:04.980
And the impact on
the cost structure

00:27:04.980 --> 00:27:11.330
is that instead of $3.5 million
for transportation cost,

00:27:11.330 --> 00:27:13.980
we now have $4 million
in transportation cost.

00:27:13.980 --> 00:27:17.000
So by adding that additional
constraint around 400 miles

00:27:17.000 --> 00:27:19.710
from your distribution
center to your customers,

00:27:19.710 --> 00:27:22.340
your total supply chain
cost rose from $7.5 million

00:27:22.340 --> 00:27:24.146
to $8 million.

00:27:24.146 --> 00:27:28.690
Let's say we actually wanted
to see the overall total cost

00:27:28.690 --> 00:27:30.880
at different number of
distribution centers.

00:27:30.880 --> 00:27:34.450
I went and ran this model
forcing it to have exactly

00:27:34.450 --> 00:27:38.380
one distribution center, exactly
two distribution centers, three

00:27:38.380 --> 00:27:40.660
distribution centers,
all the way to nine.

00:27:40.660 --> 00:27:42.730
I then recorded
the cost components

00:27:42.730 --> 00:27:45.100
between the handling
cost, the opening cost,

00:27:45.100 --> 00:27:48.240
and the transportation cost.

00:27:48.240 --> 00:27:49.830
The handling cost
in green is flat

00:27:49.830 --> 00:27:51.455
no matter how many
distribution centers

00:27:51.455 --> 00:27:53.700
you have because it's the
same volume just split

00:27:53.700 --> 00:27:55.440
in different ways.

00:27:55.440 --> 00:27:57.360
This blue line is our
distribution center

00:27:57.360 --> 00:28:00.510
opening cost, which is linear
because with more distribution

00:28:00.510 --> 00:28:03.486
centers, you're spending
another $1 million.

00:28:03.486 --> 00:28:07.125
Your transportation
cost in red goes down

00:28:07.125 --> 00:28:09.750
because as I mentioned, the more
distribution centers you have,

00:28:09.750 --> 00:28:11.750
the closer you are on
average to your customers,

00:28:11.750 --> 00:28:14.220
so your transportation
cost goes down.

00:28:14.220 --> 00:28:15.660
It goes down
dramatically from one

00:28:15.660 --> 00:28:18.310
to two distribution centers,
but then kind of flattens out.

00:28:18.310 --> 00:28:21.190
So there's not as
much of a benefit.

00:28:21.190 --> 00:28:24.780
So your total supply chain
cost, which is this purple line,

00:28:24.780 --> 00:28:27.260
is kind of a parabola.

00:28:27.260 --> 00:28:30.350
And the minimal cost occurs
at four distribution centers

00:28:30.350 --> 00:28:34.190
as we saw before like
between, for example, 2 and 5

00:28:34.190 --> 00:28:36.390
is not a tremendous
amount of difference.

00:28:36.390 --> 00:28:38.718
And so if I was doing an
actual study for a client,

00:28:38.718 --> 00:28:40.760
I'd be presenting something
like this and saying,

00:28:40.760 --> 00:28:43.040
actually, while four
distribution centers

00:28:43.040 --> 00:28:46.370
is your minimal cost
network, between 2 and 5

00:28:46.370 --> 00:28:48.290
there's not much of
a difference in cost

00:28:48.290 --> 00:28:51.720
and there's pros and
cons of each of those.

00:28:51.720 --> 00:28:52.920
All right.

00:28:52.920 --> 00:28:54.780
Well that's most of
the content folks.

00:28:54.780 --> 00:28:56.950
So really appreciate
your time today.

00:28:56.950 --> 00:29:00.510
Just wanted to wrap up by saying
and emphasizing that supply

00:29:00.510 --> 00:29:03.420
chain management really
was behind the scenes

00:29:03.420 --> 00:29:05.550
corporate function until
the beginning of the COVID

00:29:05.550 --> 00:29:08.430
pandemic, but now it's front
page news we all see it.

00:29:08.430 --> 00:29:11.100
And analytics powered by open
source programming languages,

00:29:11.100 --> 00:29:14.700
it really is changing
the nature of this field.

00:29:14.700 --> 00:29:16.132
Supply chain
network design, it's

00:29:16.132 --> 00:29:18.090
kind of obscure part of
supply chain analytics,

00:29:18.090 --> 00:29:21.152
but I find it can be a powerful
tool in devising and evaluating

00:29:21.152 --> 00:29:22.860
the organization's
supply chain strategy.

00:29:22.860 --> 00:29:25.260
And hopefully today,
you just got a taste

00:29:25.260 --> 00:29:28.880
