this app or something like it.

00:36:53.260 --> 00:36:58.090
So the SaaS that we were
using cost about $200,000

00:36:58.090 --> 00:37:02.560
per year, which for my
company is not small change.

00:37:02.560 --> 00:37:04.840
We were definitely in
a cost savings mode.

00:37:04.840 --> 00:37:08.320
It was also generating negative
number demand forecasts.

00:37:08.320 --> 00:37:10.300
So keep in mind that that's--

00:37:10.300 --> 00:37:13.680
what do you even do
with that, right?

00:37:13.680 --> 00:37:16.540
We're not going to
sell a negative number.

00:37:16.540 --> 00:37:19.030
The IT support for
this company could not

00:37:19.030 --> 00:37:20.300
figure out what was going on.

00:37:20.300 --> 00:37:23.920
So it was costing us like
a lot of time and effort

00:37:23.920 --> 00:37:27.380
to basically modify
these forecasts.

00:37:27.380 --> 00:37:32.320
So after my manager's
initial approval,

00:37:32.320 --> 00:37:34.000
I collected some demand data.

00:37:34.000 --> 00:37:37.030
We chose about 30
SKUs that we thought

00:37:37.030 --> 00:37:39.376
were representative of--

00:37:39.376 --> 00:37:41.180
of the business.

00:37:41.180 --> 00:37:43.930
And I did a rolling
forecast accuracy analysis.

00:37:43.930 --> 00:37:46.960
We wanted to have an idea before
we proceeded further what kind

00:37:46.960 --> 00:37:51.160
of accuracy we could expect
if we switch to the R forecast

00:37:51.160 --> 00:37:53.440
package that was what I
was using at the time.

00:37:53.440 --> 00:37:55.700
I'll go more into that
a little bit later.

00:37:55.700 --> 00:37:58.540
And specifically,
the ETS function,

00:37:58.540 --> 00:38:01.270
which is the exponential
smoothing commonly used

00:38:01.270 --> 00:38:03.790
in univariate supply
chain forecasting,

00:38:03.790 --> 00:38:06.140
also the ARIMA function.

00:38:06.140 --> 00:38:11.350
And there's options there
for to best fit algorithm

00:38:11.350 --> 00:38:12.470
kind of a thing.

00:38:12.470 --> 00:38:15.820
So the ETS function was actually
comparable to what was used

00:38:15.820 --> 00:38:18.490
and what we had
enabled in the SaaS,

00:38:18.490 --> 00:38:20.260
various exponential
smoothing models

00:38:20.260 --> 00:38:23.590
were selected by the
demand forecaster.

00:38:23.590 --> 00:38:27.910
So I ran this rolling
forecast accuracy analysis.

00:38:27.910 --> 00:38:35.260
And to give you an idea of
how perhaps not efficient

00:38:35.260 --> 00:38:37.360
I was at the time,
and I'm sure I'll

00:38:37.360 --> 00:38:39.910
be saying the same thing about
myself in a couple of years

00:38:39.910 --> 00:38:45.010
today, but I had three for
loops that I use to do this.

00:38:45.010 --> 00:38:49.180
And anybody who's
programmed in R for a while

00:38:49.180 --> 00:38:51.250
knows that's generally
not the best strategy.

00:38:51.250 --> 00:38:55.840
But it did work, and we
were satisfied that, hey,

00:38:55.840 --> 00:38:59.500
just doing like setting the
ETS algorithm to best fit

00:38:59.500 --> 00:39:02.530
minimizing the ICC,
that's the information

00:39:02.530 --> 00:39:05.350
criterion and the corrected
one, that was comparable to what

00:39:05.350 --> 00:39:07.540
we were getting in terms
of forecast accuracy

00:39:07.540 --> 00:39:08.980
from our SaaS.

00:39:08.980 --> 00:39:12.460
Also benefit was, hey, we
didn't need manual intervention

00:39:12.460 --> 00:39:14.950
from the demand
analyst or manager.

00:39:14.950 --> 00:39:18.980
So we made the decision to
move forward with this project

00:39:18.980 --> 00:39:23.050
and then we had to get the
buy in from the IT group

00:39:23.050 --> 00:39:26.110
at Mallinckrodt, not of
course the IT support

00:39:26.110 --> 00:39:30.295
at the SaaS company since
it was in their budget.

00:39:33.200 --> 00:39:35.690
Well, as you can
imagine, it wasn't

00:39:35.690 --> 00:39:39.110
difficult to get buy in
for a cost savings project.

00:39:39.110 --> 00:39:41.360
They were also frustrated
with the lack of support

00:39:41.360 --> 00:39:43.430
that we were getting
from the SaaS company

00:39:43.430 --> 00:39:45.770
and the time required
that we were all spending

00:39:45.770 --> 00:39:48.410
on doing useless tasks.

00:39:48.410 --> 00:39:50.900
So up till then, I had
written some other scripts

00:39:50.900 --> 00:39:53.263
I'd used for various
data wrangling tasks,

00:39:53.263 --> 00:39:55.430
the kind of stuff you do
with supply chain analysis,

00:39:55.430 --> 00:39:56.888
right, and demand
planning which is

00:39:56.888 --> 00:40:00.950
my specific area of expertise.

00:40:00.950 --> 00:40:04.730
And we didn't want to just have
me running scripts on RStudio.

00:40:04.730 --> 00:40:09.380
That's not as useful as the
free version of RStudio is.

00:40:09.380 --> 00:40:11.930
We wanted more enterprise
capable options.

00:40:11.930 --> 00:40:15.590
So we looked into
RStudio Server Pro.

00:40:15.590 --> 00:40:17.630
And I believe the
most recent version

00:40:17.630 --> 00:40:19.160
of that is called Workbench.

00:40:19.160 --> 00:40:21.180
I'm not super up
to date on that.

00:40:21.180 --> 00:40:24.378
Then we also contracted
with Lander Analytics,

00:40:24.378 --> 00:40:26.420
and they helped with server
setup and maintenance

00:40:26.420 --> 00:40:28.260
and an application installation.

00:40:28.260 --> 00:40:30.110
So it is a Linux
server for those

00:40:30.110 --> 00:40:31.850
of you might be
more Windows people,

00:40:31.850 --> 00:40:35.505
and at Mallinckrodt, we're
more of a Windows shop

00:40:35.505 --> 00:40:37.130
is what I've been
told by our IT group,

00:40:37.130 --> 00:40:40.190
so we definitely needed a little
bit more guidance in setting

00:40:40.190 --> 00:40:41.240
that up.

00:40:41.240 --> 00:40:43.640
And we had both a
production and dev server

00:40:43.640 --> 00:40:47.460
that were supported with them.

00:40:47.460 --> 00:40:47.960
OK.

00:40:47.960 --> 00:40:50.710
So we're moving forward
in this project,

00:40:50.710 --> 00:40:52.790
we made the decision
to go forward.

00:40:52.790 --> 00:40:56.360
The IT analyst who was
responsible for maintaining

00:40:56.360 --> 00:40:58.460
the data used for
stat forecasting

00:40:58.460 --> 00:41:01.370
needed to collaborate with me
to replace the data management.

00:41:01.370 --> 00:41:04.130
So not the forecasting,
but those of you

00:41:04.130 --> 00:41:05.990
might have used the
SaaS at your company

00:41:05.990 --> 00:41:08.690
for forecasting
other things, note

00:41:08.690 --> 00:41:11.060
that the SaaS can
sometimes do functions

00:41:11.060 --> 00:41:13.520
that are not just the
main function that you

00:41:13.520 --> 00:41:14.900
may have purchased it for.

00:41:14.900 --> 00:41:18.090
So we needed to do
in-house solutions.

00:41:18.090 --> 00:41:20.600
Especially generics is kind
of old school in a way,

00:41:20.600 --> 00:41:23.750
so we have like
an AS-400 system.

00:41:23.750 --> 00:41:26.428
So we needed to create
a bunch of tables,

00:41:26.428 --> 00:41:28.220
make sure we were
maintaining those tables,

00:41:28.220 --> 00:41:30.840
devs were running
that kind of thing.

00:41:30.840 --> 00:41:33.770
So we had tables for
both cleansed history

00:41:33.770 --> 00:41:35.030
and non-cleansed history.

00:41:35.030 --> 00:41:37.700
So cleansed meaning
removing outliers.

00:41:37.700 --> 00:41:40.490
Sometimes you might decide
to not include all history

00:41:40.490 --> 00:41:44.810
for consideration in
the stat forecast model

00:41:44.810 --> 00:41:47.480
because there might have been
a structural change, something

00:41:47.480 --> 00:41:48.900
like that.

00:41:48.900 --> 00:41:51.897
So we wanted to move away
from Excel spreadsheets.

00:41:51.897 --> 00:41:54.230
There is definitely a time
and a place for spreadsheets,

00:41:54.230 --> 00:41:57.260
but we just didn't feel
that was in the best

00:41:57.260 --> 00:41:59.510
interest of the business.

00:41:59.510 --> 00:42:03.110
So we used the DBI package,
which probably many of you

00:42:03.110 --> 00:42:06.740
are familiar with, and
then the odbc package--

00:42:06.740 --> 00:42:07.850
lowercase odbc.

00:42:07.850 --> 00:42:11.360
I think there's an
uppercase ROEC package.

00:42:11.360 --> 00:42:14.180
And just using some basic
SQL, we got those set up

00:42:14.180 --> 00:42:19.400
and it was very easy to
connect to the RA400 system,

00:42:19.400 --> 00:42:22.460
pull in those tables that I
would need to estimate models

00:42:22.460 --> 00:42:24.830
and calculate forecasts.

00:42:24.830 --> 00:42:27.200
We also changed our
outlier cleansing process

00:42:27.200 --> 00:42:29.540
as I alluded to in
the second point.

00:42:29.540 --> 00:42:31.490
And that is because
we had previously

00:42:31.490 --> 00:42:34.200
cleansed the history directly
in the SaaS application.

00:42:34.200 --> 00:42:36.860
So we just moved to a
pretty simple process.

00:42:36.860 --> 00:42:39.440
We were trying to come up with
something in the limited time

00:42:39.440 --> 00:42:40.340
we had.

00:42:40.340 --> 00:42:43.850
And we-- we had an Excel upload.

00:42:43.850 --> 00:42:45.710
If you needed to
remove an outlier,

00:42:45.710 --> 00:42:49.220
you'd upload an Excel
file to RA400 system.

00:42:49.220 --> 00:42:51.260
So probably could have been--

00:42:51.260 --> 00:42:53.570
well, we're changing
the process actually now

00:42:53.570 --> 00:42:55.400
and I'll get more to
that in the future.

00:42:55.400 --> 00:42:57.950
But, you know, in
the initial stages

00:42:57.950 --> 00:43:02.002
it worked, and sometimes
you have to go with that.

00:43:02.002 --> 00:43:03.710
And then we also needed
a table to source

00:43:03.710 --> 00:43:06.650
that forecast generated
from the R script so.

00:43:06.650 --> 00:43:09.140
As I said before, no
Excel files involved.

00:43:09.140 --> 00:43:11.720
We were going to
write that to a table.

00:43:11.720 --> 00:43:14.580
And then we also needed
another like a file or a table

00:43:14.580 --> 00:43:17.370
so to speak for storing
historical forecasts.

00:43:17.370 --> 00:43:20.790
And that is to measure
forecast accuracy.

00:43:20.790 --> 00:43:21.290
OK.

00:43:21.290 --> 00:43:24.500
So we were able to use RStudio
Server Pro for other uses

00:43:24.500 --> 00:43:26.090
besides stat forecasting.

00:43:26.090 --> 00:43:29.000
As I alluded to earlier
in my presentation,

00:43:29.000 --> 00:43:30.680
I had written some
other R scripts

00:43:30.680 --> 00:43:32.682
to do a lot of data
analysis and rankling tests,

00:43:32.682 --> 00:43:34.640
the kind of things that
you don't want to spend

00:43:34.640 --> 00:43:37.340
a lot of time on if you can.

00:43:37.340 --> 00:43:39.590
Measuring weighted
MAPE-- weighted MAPE

00:43:39.590 --> 00:43:42.000
is what we use to measure
our forecast accuracy.

00:43:42.000 --> 00:43:46.130
There are other metrics that
your organization might use.

00:43:46.130 --> 00:43:48.500
Checking the commercial
forecasts upload

00:43:48.500 --> 00:43:51.110
versus the download.

00:43:51.110 --> 00:43:53.690
I am responsible for loading
the commercial partners

00:43:53.690 --> 00:43:55.460
forecast to the ERP system.

00:43:55.460 --> 00:43:58.820
And depending on
how things go, we

00:43:58.820 --> 00:44:01.460
might have kind of phantom
forecasts hanging out

00:44:01.460 --> 00:44:02.880
in the ERP system.

00:44:02.880 --> 00:44:06.650
So it's hard to check that
in just doing a spreadsheet

00:44:06.650 --> 00:44:09.590
analysis, but it was
fairly simple to write

00:44:09.590 --> 00:44:12.110
a program that did it in R.

00:44:12.110 --> 00:44:15.500
Also, last year with
the pand-- well,

00:44:15.500 --> 00:44:19.520
I said last year because it's
two years ago almost now,

00:44:19.520 --> 00:44:23.090
we had a lot of orders
for acetaminophen

00:44:23.090 --> 00:44:25.280
with the start of the pandemic.

00:44:25.280 --> 00:44:28.940
And our product director needed
some help in understanding

00:44:28.940 --> 00:44:33.895
how to allocate orders
and versus forecast.

00:44:33.895 --> 00:44:35.270
So we had assortment
of products.

00:44:35.270 --> 00:44:37.490
And based on the forecast
the customer provided,

00:44:37.490 --> 00:44:39.365
they were either going
to get product or have

00:44:39.365 --> 00:44:42.030
to kind of go to the end
of the line, so to speak.

00:44:42.030 --> 00:44:44.700
And there were some other
one for the sake of time,

00:44:44.700 --> 00:44:47.310
I won't bore you guys
with the details of those.

00:44:47.310 --> 00:44:49.520
So it was very-- it was
simple to set these jobs up

00:44:49.520 --> 00:44:53.390
to run on the server
using CronR And that's,

00:44:53.390 --> 00:44:55.130
as you would expect
by the name, it's

00:44:55.130 --> 00:44:59.140
an R package which
interfaces with Cron.

00:44:59.140 --> 00:45:01.520
So the script saved a lot
of time all these jobs set.

00:45:01.520 --> 00:45:03.560
Up we could compile
a lot of data

00:45:03.560 --> 00:45:08.110
reports with no intervention,
or maybe a little bit of just

00:45:08.110 --> 00:45:11.540
give it a check from
demand managers.

00:45:11.540 --> 00:45:14.000
And then the real test is
when I was out on short term

00:45:14.000 --> 00:45:17.000
disability this past summer.

00:45:17.000 --> 00:45:18.740
The jobs ran well.

00:45:18.740 --> 00:45:20.240
They saved my
coworker a lot of time

00:45:20.240 --> 00:45:24.227
having to cover my
responsibility as well as his.

00:45:24.227 --> 00:45:26.060
So my coworker left the
company in November,

00:45:26.060 --> 00:45:29.600
so I've since been taking
over his former duties.

00:45:29.600 --> 00:45:33.800
And I've been writing various
R scripts to kind of hopefully

00:45:33.800 --> 00:45:35.580
improve some of the processes.

00:45:35.580 --> 00:45:37.130
And it really has
made it possible

00:45:37.130 --> 00:45:42.185
scheduling these jobs on the
RStudio Server Pro interface.

00:45:42.185 --> 00:45:46.040
It has really made it possible
for me to do my job as well

00:45:46.040 --> 00:45:49.350
as his previous duties

00:45:49.350 --> 00:45:49.850
OK.

00:45:49.850 --> 00:45:52.010
To summarize the
project, you're probably

00:45:52.010 --> 00:45:53.884
wondering how long
did all this take.

00:45:53.884 --> 00:45:55.850
It took about 11 months.

00:45:55.850 --> 00:45:58.940
And I will say this.

00:45:58.940 --> 00:46:00.800
You could probably do
this kind of project

00:46:00.800 --> 00:46:02.510
where you totally
replace the software

00:46:02.510 --> 00:46:05.750
you've been using in less time.

00:46:05.750 --> 00:46:07.700
We happened to
have a lot of time

00:46:07.700 --> 00:46:10.380
based on when the contract with
the SaaS was going to be up.

00:46:10.380 --> 00:46:12.950
And so we had several months
in early 2021 where we

00:46:12.950 --> 00:46:17.060
were running both concurrently.

00:46:17.060 --> 00:46:18.590
There are definitely
improvements

00:46:18.590 --> 00:46:20.300
to be made to the
forecasting processes,

00:46:20.300 --> 00:46:22.280
and I can tell you
that as well as anybody

00:46:22.280 --> 00:46:25.850
at the company, that's what
I deal with day in day out.

00:46:25.850 --> 00:46:28.790
But the initial goal
of replacing the SaaS

00:46:28.790 --> 00:46:31.470
with solution at least as
effective was completed.

00:46:31.470 --> 00:46:34.830
So we consider that the
initial stage to be a success.

00:46:34.830 --> 00:46:38.630
We measure on a LAG 3 forecast
accuracy, which basically takes

00:46:38.630 --> 00:46:40.010
into consideration--
essentially,

00:46:40.010 --> 00:46:42.500
it's sort of like LAG 4
if you think about it.

00:46:42.500 --> 00:46:44.270
Takes history from
four months ago

00:46:44.270 --> 00:46:48.110
when you're measuring the
particular month in question.

00:46:48.110 --> 00:46:49.490
But it's been about the same.

00:46:49.490 --> 00:46:51.380
Depending on the month,
a little bit worse,

00:46:51.380 --> 00:46:52.760
a few percentage points.

00:46:52.760 --> 00:46:54.830
But we did meet
our goal for 2021.

00:46:54.830 --> 00:46:57.710
So my boss is happy, I'm happy.

00:46:57.710 --> 00:47:00.320
And the cost savings
is definitely

00:47:00.320 --> 00:47:03.350
worth it for a slight
trade often accuracy.

00:47:03.350 --> 00:47:07.580
And we are still using pretty
simple models like ARIMA

00:47:07.580 --> 00:47:08.990
exponential smoothing models.

00:47:08.990 --> 00:47:12.230
So we hope that as we
introduce new models,

00:47:12.230 --> 00:47:16.470
we will improve
accuracy over time.

00:47:16.470 --> 00:47:20.430
And then I alluded to this
earlier in the presentation.

00:47:20.430 --> 00:47:22.790
So I started out with
the forecast package

00:47:22.790 --> 00:47:24.560
in summer 2020.

00:47:24.560 --> 00:47:26.480
And then I found out--

00:47:26.480 --> 00:47:28.940
I guess it was probably old
news but it was new news to me

00:47:28.940 --> 00:47:31.980
at that time about the
tidyverts suite of packages,

00:47:31.980 --> 00:47:34.640
which is kind of the replacement
for the forecast package.

00:47:34.640 --> 00:47:36.410
And so I switched to that.

00:47:36.410 --> 00:47:39.050
I switched over the code
that I had written to that.

00:47:39.050 --> 00:47:40.610
It took a little
bit to kind of make

00:47:40.610 --> 00:47:41.570
sure I understood everything.

00:47:41.570 --> 00:47:43.160
But it's a great
suite of packages

00:47:43.160 --> 00:47:48.193
that includes stable
fees, stable data I think.

00:47:48.193 --> 00:47:49.610
So I have found
those to be really

00:47:49.610 --> 00:47:52.370
useful for automatic forecasting
if you have a lot of SKUs

00:47:52.370 --> 00:47:55.220
and you don't have time to
go in and tweak parameters,

00:47:55.220 --> 00:47:56.750
figure out the
best reader model,

00:47:56.750 --> 00:47:59.120
figure out the best
exponential smoothing model,

00:47:59.120 --> 00:48:02.210
for instance like
univariate forecasting.

00:48:02.210 --> 00:48:04.820
The models, they produce--
the forecast for supply chain

00:48:04.820 --> 00:48:06.710
are stable forecasts.

00:48:06.710 --> 00:48:08.520
They don't require a
lot of intervention,

00:48:08.520 --> 00:48:14.780
you're not going to get anything
unbelievable or crazy I guess.

00:48:14.780 --> 00:48:15.280
OK.

00:48:15.280 --> 00:48:18.257
So I mentioned the
Shiny app earlier.

00:48:18.257 --> 00:48:20.590
So the impetus of the project
started with a simple time

00:48:20.590 --> 00:48:22.420
series forecasting app.

00:48:22.420 --> 00:48:25.120
We made the decision to
forecast the script scheduled

00:48:25.120 --> 00:48:27.520
as a monthly job
instead of an app.

00:48:27.520 --> 00:48:29.950
And part of the reason
for that seemed simpler

00:48:29.950 --> 00:48:32.710
at the time, time savings.

00:48:32.710 --> 00:48:34.180
Also, it ended up working out.

00:48:34.180 --> 00:48:36.670
We weren't really ready
to go with RStudio Connect

00:48:36.670 --> 00:48:38.440
at the time, which
we would have needed

00:48:38.440 --> 00:48:43.590
to host the app for an
enterprise capable way.

00:48:43.590 --> 00:48:46.280
So after that, I developed
another Shiny app

00:48:46.280 --> 00:48:49.490
kind of based on my first
one, which is more--

00:48:49.490 --> 00:48:51.680
uses a number of
different packages,

00:48:51.680 --> 00:48:53.570
the tidyverts
packages of course,

00:48:53.570 --> 00:48:57.470
DT, I think Ralph mentioned that
in his app, to edit history,

00:48:57.470 --> 00:48:59.330
you can change parameters.

00:48:59.330 --> 00:49:03.440
Basically interfaces with
RA400 system via SQL.

00:49:03.440 --> 00:49:04.820
So you can--
through the app, you

00:49:04.820 --> 00:49:10.820
can interactively edit our
data in our company systems.

00:49:10.820 --> 00:49:12.470
And this allows
me to, especially

00:49:12.470 --> 00:49:14.330
for those high volume
really important

00:49:14.330 --> 00:49:17.180
SKUs, to go in and
pick out specifications

00:49:17.180 --> 00:49:18.240
I think are optimal.

00:49:18.240 --> 00:49:20.390
So we're hoping that
this will give us

00:49:20.390 --> 00:49:25.400
more opportunity to kind of
tune a better model for the SKUs

00:49:25.400 --> 00:49:29.950
that we consider to
be A SKUs important.

00:49:29.950 --> 00:49:31.540
The impetus for
creating this app

00:49:31.540 --> 00:49:34.150
was actually my former coworker.

00:49:34.150 --> 00:49:36.220
And he wanted
something that mimics

00:49:36.220 --> 00:49:38.850
some of the functionality that
we had in our previous SaaS.

00:49:38.850 --> 00:49:42.040
So he liked being able to
go in, play with parameters,

00:49:42.040 --> 00:49:43.660
kind of test things out.

00:49:43.660 --> 00:49:45.520
See of how he could
change the history, how

00:49:45.520 --> 00:49:47.780
does this change the
forecast, that kind of thing,

00:49:47.780 --> 00:49:49.930
look at different
accuracy metrics

00:49:49.930 --> 00:49:54.960
as he was creating his forecast.

00:49:54.960 --> 00:49:56.428
So future state.

00:49:56.428 --> 00:49:58.970
It's always good to be thinking
about continuous improvement,

00:49:58.970 --> 00:49:59.510
right?

00:49:59.510 --> 00:50:03.560
So I'm working on
another Shiny app

00:50:03.560 --> 00:50:06.620
to visualize our generic data,
that's our finished dosage

00:50:06.620 --> 00:50:12.200
data with the pivot table
probably using the pivot tabler

00:50:12.200 --> 00:50:12.710
package.

00:50:12.710 --> 00:50:13.430
Not sure yet.

00:50:13.430 --> 00:50:15.530
I might look into a
few other packages.

00:50:15.530 --> 00:50:18.620
And then we either look
at monthly or weekly doses

00:50:18.620 --> 00:50:21.500
or bottles depending on
what the user would want.

00:50:21.500 --> 00:50:24.350
So I currently use
this very slow tool

00:50:24.350 --> 00:50:26.990
using a legacy system
I will not name.

00:50:26.990 --> 00:50:31.700
And we use this for creating
visualizations for the demand

00:50:31.700 --> 00:50:34.790
review slide deck, where we
look at our customer demand

00:50:34.790 --> 00:50:37.220
compare that to our--

00:50:37.220 --> 00:50:39.255
look at our forecast
to see what's changed,

00:50:39.255 --> 00:50:41.130
look at our financials,
that kind of a thing.

00:50:41.130 --> 00:50:43.700
So this will helped me
more than anybody else,

00:50:43.700 --> 00:50:47.690
but I'm definitely interested
in improving my processes.

00:50:47.690 --> 00:50:49.190
And then another
project we started

00:50:49.190 --> 00:50:52.910
last month is looking at the
commercial team forecasts.

00:50:52.910 --> 00:50:56.300
So they currently don't use
much statistical forecasting.

00:50:56.300 --> 00:50:59.120
So they are interested though
in looking at their end purchase

00:50:59.120 --> 00:51:01.220
or demand and how
we can roll that up

00:51:01.220 --> 00:51:03.740
under distributor customers.

00:51:03.740 --> 00:51:07.310
Mallinckrodt is a manufacturer,
so we sell to some big time

00:51:07.310 --> 00:51:10.520
distributor customers you may
have heard of in the news.

00:51:10.520 --> 00:51:12.980
And there are certain customers
that are pricing contracts,

00:51:12.980 --> 00:51:14.480
and then those
distributor customers

00:51:14.480 --> 00:51:18.072
sell to more of the retail
pharmacies kind of a thing.

00:51:18.072 --> 00:51:20.280
So we're hoping to get a
better picture of our demand

00:51:20.280 --> 00:51:23.210
to kind of tie that
out and build a better

00:51:23.210 --> 00:51:26.460
forecast for them in that way.

00:51:26.460 --> 00:51:29.030
And then in terms of
model improvement,

00:51:29.030 --> 00:51:32.660
those of you who are familiar
with the tidyverts packages

00:51:32.660 --> 00:51:34.970
probably know that you can
do hierarchical forecasting

00:51:34.970 --> 00:51:35.780
as well.

00:51:35.780 --> 00:51:38.480
We did explore that a little
bit in the initial stages

00:51:38.480 --> 00:51:40.730
of the project,
excuse me, when we

00:51:40.730 --> 00:51:45.020
were trying to understand
what we wanted,

00:51:45.020 --> 00:51:48.320
how much we wanted to experiment
with different kinds of models.

00:51:48.320 --> 00:51:50.570
My boss thought it wasn't
a good idea at the time,

00:51:50.570 --> 00:51:52.790
but now that we've
completed the initial stage,

00:51:52.790 --> 00:51:55.160
we'll probably look into
that a little bit more.

00:51:55.160 --> 00:51:59.163
And then also machine learning
methods for time series.

00:51:59.163 --> 00:52:00.830
We would need to do
that on weekly data.

00:52:00.830 --> 00:52:02.270
We don't have enough
monthly data really

00:52:02.270 --> 00:52:03.103
to make it worth it.

00:52:03.103 --> 00:52:05.990
But model time is
a good package.

00:52:05.990 --> 00:52:10.190
So that is something that
probably this year we

00:52:10.190 --> 00:52:13.380
are looking into exploring.

00:52:13.380 --> 00:52:17.150
So to summarize, I just--
a lot of on this slide

00:52:17.150 --> 00:52:21.080
is common sense but I think
it's probably worth reiterating.

00:52:21.080 --> 00:52:23.540
You're interested in
doing like a project where

00:52:23.540 --> 00:52:28.790
you replace a SaaS
with an R solution,

00:52:28.790 --> 00:52:31.670
start sooner versus later.

00:52:31.670 --> 00:52:34.850
Something that we learned
sometimes the hard way

00:52:34.850 --> 00:52:37.850
is that the SaaS will
perform other functions

00:52:37.850 --> 00:52:40.070
besides forecasting.

00:52:40.070 --> 00:52:42.740
So you need to come up with
solutions for data storage

00:52:42.740 --> 00:52:43.640
and management.

00:52:43.640 --> 00:52:47.030
And those solutions
might be R specific.

00:52:47.030 --> 00:52:50.270
They need to be compatible
with whatever you're

00:52:50.270 --> 00:52:52.920
doing in R for forecasting.

00:52:52.920 --> 00:52:55.310
So you might need to create
a database, a data warehouse,

00:52:55.310 --> 00:52:57.060
maybe you already have
something like that

00:52:57.060 --> 00:52:58.880
at your company or
your organization.

00:52:58.880 --> 00:53:02.390
Also project
management 101 I guess,

00:53:02.390 --> 00:53:04.970
make sure you beat estimate
the time involved on your part

00:53:04.970 --> 00:53:08.540
as well as many collaborators
because you don't want

00:53:08.540 --> 00:53:10.040
to come up to the
end of the project

00:53:10.040 --> 00:53:12.680
and figure out that they don't
have enough time to give you--

00:53:12.680 --> 00:53:14.780
to complete the project.

00:53:14.780 --> 00:53:18.350
And then I wish somebody had
told me this ahead of time.

00:53:18.350 --> 00:53:21.230
Learning some project
management skills is helpful.

00:53:21.230 --> 00:53:23.000
Probably a lot of
us on this call

00:53:23.000 --> 00:53:26.520
have good technical skills.

00:53:26.520 --> 00:53:28.610
When I was trying to map
out this process which

00:53:28.610 --> 00:53:32.690
is my subsequent point,
I had really no idea

00:53:32.690 --> 00:53:33.660
what I was doing.

00:53:33.660 --> 00:53:39.470
And it got done, but it could
have been a lot more efficient.

00:53:39.470 --> 00:53:43.520
And then I feel like I might be
a little bit of a broken record

00:53:43.520 --> 00:53:45.980
here, but you don't
want to realize

00:53:45.980 --> 00:53:48.050
you have an account for
something you previously

00:53:48.050 --> 00:53:49.940
relied on the SaaS to
do when you've almost

00:53:49.940 --> 00:53:51.350
completed the project.

00:53:51.350 --> 00:53:54.710
And as you probably expect,
we learned the hard way.

00:53:54.710 --> 00:53:56.720
My coworker used the
SaaS for something.

00:53:56.720 --> 00:53:59.340
I did not exactly know
what he was doing.

00:53:59.340 --> 00:54:01.790
And I think there was
a miscommunication

00:54:01.790 --> 00:54:06.620
between perhaps an
IT analyst and him.

00:54:06.620 --> 00:54:10.010
Just throwing blame, but
we realized oh, we never

00:54:10.010 --> 00:54:11.090
accounted for this.

00:54:11.090 --> 00:54:13.800
Now we're practically
finished with this project

00:54:13.800 --> 00:54:15.800
and we need to make
sure we have something

00:54:15.800 --> 00:54:18.500
that will allow him to do
what he did with the SaaS.

00:54:18.500 --> 00:54:20.930
So you don't want
to-- you don't want

00:54:20.930 --> 00:54:23.060
to have our experience there.

00:54:23.060 --> 00:54:25.040
And then I I'll finish.

00:54:25.040 --> 00:54:27.950
Hopefully this is not
too pessimistic a note,

00:54:27.950 --> 00:54:31.190
but with a paraphrasing
of Murphy's law,

00:54:31.190 --> 00:54:33.410
something can go
wrong, it will, so

00:54:33.410 --> 00:54:35.180
plan for all the contingencies.

00:54:35.180 --> 00:54:37.340
And since we're all in
supply chain planning,

00:54:37.340 --> 00:54:39.480
that shouldn't be too hard.

00:54:39.480 --> 00:54:39.980
OK.

00:54:39.980 --> 00:54:41.240
That's all I've got.

00:54:41.240 --> 00:54:43.961
Questions I guess Rachel?

00:54:43.961 --> 00:54:45.628
I don't know how we're
going to do this.

00:54:45.628 --> 00:54:46.490
RACHEL: Yeah.

00:54:46.490 --> 00:54:47.862
I can help with reading this.

00:54:47.862 --> 00:54:49.070
But thank you so much, Laura.

00:54:49.070 --> 00:54:51.020
That was an awesome
presentation.

00:54:51.020 --> 00:54:52.640
Really appreciate it.

