00:57:39.460 --> 00:57:42.160
But Eduardo, I saw you had
a great question that you

00:57:42.160 --> 00:57:44.180
put into the Slido as well.

00:57:44.180 --> 00:57:46.810
And I was wondering if you'd
want to read that one out loud

00:57:46.810 --> 00:57:48.310
and add some context there.

00:57:52.170 --> 00:57:56.670
And I can start by
reading the question too.

00:57:56.670 --> 00:58:01.500
But it was Laura, did you have
to deal with uploading back

00:58:01.500 --> 00:58:03.810
the forecast to ERP?

00:58:03.810 --> 00:58:06.240
If that was the case,
were there any guidelines

00:58:06.240 --> 00:58:09.645
you had to follow with IT?

00:58:09.645 --> 00:58:12.180
LAURA ROSE: So you're talking
to stat forecast I assume?

00:58:12.180 --> 00:58:15.730
I mean, I know I mentioned
doing a check of an upload.

00:58:15.730 --> 00:58:17.700
So I'm assuming you're
going with stat forecast

00:58:17.700 --> 00:58:20.940
because that's what the models
estimated then calculate in R.

00:58:20.940 --> 00:58:23.730
So I actually worked-- the
nice thing about designing

00:58:23.730 --> 00:58:26.890
the system on your own, again,
maybe I'm a control freak

00:58:26.890 --> 00:58:29.220
but I hope not, is
that I had a chance

00:58:29.220 --> 00:58:30.977
to work with the IT analyst.

00:58:30.977 --> 00:58:33.060
And he and I have a very
good working relationship

00:58:33.060 --> 00:58:35.460
to say OK, this is the kind
of table we want to build.

00:58:35.460 --> 00:58:37.870
I need this field, I need
this field, I need this field.

00:58:37.870 --> 00:58:41.760
So we kind of have a
custom solution built.

00:58:41.760 --> 00:58:44.190
I think within my--

00:58:44.190 --> 00:58:46.950
within the service account,
which we use in RStudio,

00:58:46.950 --> 00:58:48.550
it's not tied to my
personal account,

00:58:48.550 --> 00:58:51.810
so it can run if I'm on short
term disability or whatever.

00:58:54.630 --> 00:58:58.380
I think we had to have like
permissions set so that anybody

00:58:58.380 --> 00:59:01.470
you could write with that
account to the table.

00:59:01.470 --> 00:59:03.120
A pretty simple fix though.

00:59:03.120 --> 00:59:06.540
So no, they were basically
like what do you need from IT,

00:59:06.540 --> 00:59:09.270
and I was like, here's what I
want, here's where I want it.

00:59:09.270 --> 00:59:12.277
They said OK.

00:59:12.277 --> 00:59:12.860
RACHEL: Great.

00:59:12.860 --> 00:59:14.990
Thanks Laura.

00:59:14.990 --> 00:59:17.510
One other question that
came through on Slido

00:59:17.510 --> 00:59:20.810
was how do you store
snapshots of your forecast

00:59:20.810 --> 00:59:23.625
for later accuracy analysis?

00:59:23.625 --> 00:59:24.500
LAURA ROSE: OK, yeah.

00:59:24.500 --> 00:59:26.690
I kind of did allude
to that I guess.

00:59:31.100 --> 00:59:32.900
This is something
that IT team handles.

00:59:32.900 --> 00:59:34.850
I guess I could
theoretically do this in R.

00:59:34.850 --> 00:59:38.720
But at the end of the
month, before the table that

00:59:38.720 --> 00:59:45.790
receives the forecast is
cleared that forecast,

00:59:45.790 --> 00:59:49.720
so all 67,000 now lines
of it or whatever,

00:59:49.720 --> 00:59:52.930
we forecast three years out.

00:59:52.930 --> 00:59:55.510
Nothing magical about three
years, just how we do it.

00:59:55.510 --> 00:59:57.670
It's copied to
another table that

00:59:57.670 --> 01:00:01.150
has I think we saw 13 months
of historical forecasts.

01:00:01.150 --> 01:00:04.060
And then what I measure
statistical MAPE,

01:00:04.060 --> 01:00:07.660
the script pulls in from that
historical table filters.

01:00:07.660 --> 01:00:11.180
I use some of the
DVI interfaces,

01:00:11.180 --> 01:00:15.100
and in the parameter
argument, you

01:00:15.100 --> 01:00:17.790
can say I want to pull in
based on this criteria.

01:00:17.790 --> 01:00:20.800
Can you pull in whatever
might be your LAG 3

01:00:20.800 --> 01:00:22.150
forecast or LAG 1.

01:00:22.150 --> 01:00:23.590
So that's how we measure it.

01:00:23.590 --> 01:00:27.640
But yeah, we do track our
stat forecast accuracy

01:00:27.640 --> 01:00:29.307
pretty carefully.

01:00:29.307 --> 01:00:29.890
RACHEL: Great.

01:00:29.890 --> 01:00:31.540
Thank you so much, Laura.

01:00:31.540 --> 01:00:34.090
And I see there are
a ton of questions

01:00:34.090 --> 01:00:35.510
that have come in here too.

01:00:35.510 --> 01:00:37.900
So a few of them
don't say if it's

01:00:37.900 --> 01:00:41.240
for Ralph or Laura
either or both.

01:00:41.240 --> 01:00:44.350
So I'd love to throw
some questions at you

01:00:44.350 --> 01:00:47.360
as well Ralph from earlier too.

01:00:47.360 --> 01:00:50.770
But one was, Ralph,
what R packages

01:00:50.770 --> 01:00:55.330
do you use for operations
research optimization problems?

01:00:55.330 --> 01:00:56.080
RALPH ASHER: Yeah.

01:00:56.080 --> 01:00:59.260
So for optimization,
the biggest ones I use

01:00:59.260 --> 01:01:04.300
is the ompr, which stands for
I think optimization modeling

01:01:04.300 --> 01:01:07.270
process in R. I'm
not sure about the P.

01:01:07.270 --> 01:01:10.840
And essentially, it's
a pretty intuitive way

01:01:10.840 --> 01:01:13.390
to take one of your program
or any of your program

01:01:13.390 --> 01:01:16.494
optimization models and
formulate them in R.

01:01:16.494 --> 01:01:20.290
And that's kind of
modeling language package.

01:01:20.290 --> 01:01:22.120
And then there's some
adjacent packages

01:01:22.120 --> 01:01:24.860
that actually then send it
to the solver of your choice.

01:01:24.860 --> 01:01:27.850
And so if you go on the
packages at GitHub site,

01:01:27.850 --> 01:01:30.070
it's very easy to read.

01:01:30.070 --> 01:01:33.370
But I like that
because it's built

01:01:33.370 --> 01:01:35.660
with a tidyverse
centric mindset.

01:01:35.660 --> 01:01:39.710
So for example, you use pipes
to add components of your model.

01:01:39.710 --> 01:01:42.220
And you can do
whatever code you need

01:01:42.220 --> 01:01:44.860
to do to prep your input
tables and then just send it

01:01:44.860 --> 01:01:46.870
into your optimization
model, which

01:01:46.870 --> 01:01:49.750
is how I'm able to kind of
dynamically change the inputs

01:01:49.750 --> 01:01:52.060
and then react demise
on the back end.

01:01:52.060 --> 01:01:55.830
That's probably what I use.

