01:07:52.100 --> 01:07:55.320
So a few other questions here.

01:07:55.320 --> 01:07:57.230
So for Ralph, there is one.

01:07:57.230 --> 01:08:00.140
How do you incorporate
uncertain inputs

01:08:00.140 --> 01:08:02.570
like Laura's demand
forecast into models

01:08:02.570 --> 01:08:05.683
to be run in
deterministic solvers?

01:08:05.683 --> 01:08:07.350
RALPH ASHER: Yeah,
it's a good question.

01:08:07.350 --> 01:08:11.790
So this is kind of where the
R and science come together.

01:08:11.790 --> 01:08:14.120
And so there's a couple of ways.

01:08:14.120 --> 01:08:19.279
The way I primarily like to use
is essentially use simulation

01:08:19.279 --> 01:08:21.450
to change the inputs.

01:08:21.450 --> 01:08:25.609
So for example, if the
demand for Ames, Iowa

01:08:25.609 --> 01:08:27.319
was not this number,
but rather we

01:08:27.319 --> 01:08:29.569
think it's from here
to here, you kind of

01:08:29.569 --> 01:08:33.140
do a sampling of each of those
and you run the optimization.

01:08:33.140 --> 01:08:36.200
And overall, you do
this a bunch of times

01:08:36.200 --> 01:08:40.000
to kind of compare the outputs
and see how different really

01:08:40.000 --> 01:08:41.020
are the recommendations.

01:08:41.020 --> 01:08:44.979
Like if it's always recommending
these four warehouses,

01:08:44.979 --> 01:08:47.779
those four warehouses
are a good choice.

01:08:47.779 --> 01:08:49.620
If there is radically
different outputs,

01:08:49.620 --> 01:08:53.210
then you kind of got to
dig a little bit deeper.

01:08:53.210 --> 01:08:55.450
Thanks Ralph.

01:08:55.450 --> 01:08:59.736
I see-- another question was--

01:08:59.736 --> 01:09:01.569
it could be for either
