01:09:17.950 --> 01:09:20.420
If you are able to get
a little more color

01:09:20.420 --> 01:09:22.479
and put that in the
chat or somewhere else,

01:09:22.479 --> 01:09:26.140
perhaps Rachel can give
me some insight into that.

01:09:26.140 --> 01:09:30.040
Because we use statistical
forecasting, we were using it

01:09:30.040 --> 01:09:31.990
I was going to say,
it's more of a benchmark

01:09:31.990 --> 01:09:35.500
for our commercial
partners, there wasn't

01:09:35.500 --> 01:09:37.779
a lot of compliance
present in our pharma,

01:09:37.779 --> 01:09:41.729
especially opiates, which
is one of the products

01:09:41.729 --> 01:09:42.729
that Mallinckrodt makes.

01:09:42.729 --> 01:09:44.920
It's very highly
regulated as you know.

01:09:44.920 --> 01:09:49.390
But the commercial
teams, their forecast

01:09:49.390 --> 01:09:51.460
has to submit a lot
more customer level

01:09:51.460 --> 01:09:54.130
evidence to the DEA for quota.

01:09:54.130 --> 01:09:55.930
For statistical
forecasts, we're basically

01:09:55.930 --> 01:09:57.280
saying what is our demand say.

01:09:57.280 --> 01:09:59.500
So there wasn't as much
of a compliance issue.

01:09:59.500 --> 01:10:01.660
And my company, I will
say for being a fairly

01:10:01.660 --> 01:10:04.850
large organization, people
are pretty agile about hey,

01:10:04.850 --> 01:10:06.320
if something works
and we like it,

01:10:06.320 --> 01:10:08.403
we're going with it as
long as we're not obviously

01:10:08.403 --> 01:10:11.740
breaking any laws or
circumventing regulations.

01:10:11.740 --> 01:10:15.070
So if you have any more
compliance-- like I see someone

01:10:15.070 --> 01:10:19.330
says compliance
with IT standards.

01:10:19.330 --> 01:10:22.240
Yeah, I mean they were--

01:10:22.240 --> 01:10:25.600
I'm very fortunate to have
an I really chill IT team.

01:10:25.600 --> 01:10:28.480
But they were like,
yeah, let's go for it.

01:10:28.480 --> 01:10:30.830
It sounds good, as long as
they get the time to do it,

01:10:30.830 --> 01:10:33.127
which they did.

01:10:33.127 --> 01:10:34.960
And then I see someone
else said compliance,

01:10:34.960 --> 01:10:37.820
my financial division
will kill me if I do that.

01:10:37.820 --> 01:10:41.620
Well, I mean, I go back
to my previous comment.

01:10:41.620 --> 01:10:45.440
If you can simplify their lives,
maybe they would reconsider.

01:10:45.440 --> 01:10:49.260
So hopefully that's helpful.

01:10:49.260 --> 01:10:53.120
I like that idea of
simplifying other people's

01:10:53.120 --> 01:10:56.840
lives to push things forward.

01:10:56.840 --> 01:10:58.400
Another upvoted
question was what

01:10:58.400 --> 01:11:01.940
metrics do you use to
measure forecast accuracy?

01:11:01.940 --> 01:11:04.228
And I'm not sure if you
had covered this at all.

01:11:04.228 --> 01:11:06.020
LAURA ROSE: Yeah, I
guess I briefly touched

01:11:06.020 --> 01:11:07.760
on it in my presentation.

01:11:07.760 --> 01:11:09.992
We use weighted MAPE.

01:11:09.992 --> 01:11:12.450
How we define that, there's a
couple different definitions,

01:11:12.450 --> 01:11:16.770
but it's basically the sum
of of the absolute variances.

01:11:16.770 --> 01:11:18.350
So we measure it at SKU level.

01:11:18.350 --> 01:11:20.820
You can do it at
different levels.

01:11:20.820 --> 01:11:24.200
Forecast minus demand or
absolute forecast minus

01:11:24.200 --> 01:11:26.180
demand over the sum
of all the demand.

01:11:26.180 --> 01:11:29.030
So that essentially
gives more weight

01:11:29.030 --> 01:11:34.520
to higher volume SKUs
relatively speaking.

01:11:34.520 --> 01:11:37.190
I mean, it's all aggregated,
but we don't really

01:11:37.190 --> 01:11:39.320
pay attention as much to
the little volume SKUs

01:11:39.320 --> 01:11:41.840
because, hey, if they're
really accurate or they're not,

01:11:41.840 --> 01:11:45.740
their absolute variances that
aren't going to be that big.

01:11:45.740 --> 01:11:47.022
Thanks Laura.

