01:25:56.850 --> 01:25:58.530
that you read in
that survey data

01:25:58.530 --> 01:26:01.020
and assign a
numerical weighting.

01:26:01.020 --> 01:26:05.910
You could use that or
other kind of feedback

01:26:05.910 --> 01:26:09.660
from perhaps a website
or something else.

01:26:09.660 --> 01:26:11.610
I haven't worked in
consumer packaged

01:26:11.610 --> 01:26:14.828
goods for several
years, so I'm not super

01:26:14.828 --> 01:26:16.620
up on whatever might
be the new techniques,

01:26:16.620 --> 01:26:18.960
but those are a couple of
ideas that come to my mind.

01:26:21.920 --> 01:26:24.740
Thanks Laura.

01:26:24.740 --> 01:26:26.780
Vesalius, I did see
your hand was raised,

01:26:26.780 --> 01:26:28.940
so I love to just have
that be the last question

01:26:28.940 --> 01:26:31.928
too if you want to jump in.

01:26:31.928 --> 01:26:32.720
Thank you Rachel.

01:26:32.720 --> 01:26:35.120
And thank you both
to Ralph and Laura

01:26:35.120 --> 01:26:37.290
for our great presentations.

01:26:37.290 --> 01:26:41.240
So just a very quick question
to anyone who's actually

01:26:41.240 --> 01:26:43.370
relevant to the subject.

01:26:43.370 --> 01:26:46.340
Have you also used-- have
you also measured bias

01:26:46.340 --> 01:26:49.670
to optimize your forecasts
or just accuracy measures?

01:26:49.670 --> 01:26:53.750
And if you did, how
did you do that?

01:26:53.750 --> 01:26:55.850
NAR, outside the bar?

01:26:55.850 --> 01:26:58.130
Just some basics.

01:26:58.130 --> 01:27:00.570
Yeah, yeah.

01:27:00.570 --> 01:27:02.950
I missed that question
in the chat earlier,

01:27:02.950 --> 01:27:04.380
so thank you for asking that.

01:27:04.380 --> 01:27:06.620
We do measure bias.

01:27:06.620 --> 01:27:10.580
Well we have-- so bias for I
think perhaps maybe on the call

01:27:10.580 --> 01:27:13.370
notice, but it's basically
the variance over the demand.

01:27:13.370 --> 01:27:14.550
So some of the variances.

01:27:14.550 --> 01:27:16.640
So that is basically
directionally,

01:27:16.640 --> 01:27:18.585
you under forecast
or over forecast.

01:27:18.585 --> 01:27:20.210
It's actually a big
point of discussion

01:27:20.210 --> 01:27:22.490
right now for our
commercial partners.

01:27:22.490 --> 01:27:25.220
For stat forecasts, we're
more focused on accuracy.

01:27:25.220 --> 01:27:29.230
But yeah, if you have
the data and how we--

01:27:29.230 --> 01:27:30.740
we use a lot of Excel.

01:27:30.740 --> 01:27:33.140
My coworkers love
Excel spreadsheets,

01:27:33.140 --> 01:27:34.880
these giant spreadsheets.

01:27:34.880 --> 01:27:37.110
Hey, it is what it is,
so I work with that.

01:27:37.110 --> 01:27:41.660
Open XLSX, say that five times
fast, is a great package.

01:27:41.660 --> 01:27:44.637
But I can write that data
in and we do track it.

01:27:44.637 --> 01:27:46.970
And it's important because I
think a lot of times people

01:27:46.970 --> 01:27:50.330
do get focused on
accuracy and then--

01:27:50.330 --> 01:27:52.910
I actually think that
one of our divisions,

01:27:52.910 --> 01:27:56.060
and they have this
consistent bias.

01:27:56.060 --> 01:28:00.273
But we're only like
5% weighted MAPE.

01:28:00.273 --> 01:28:01.190
It's like we're great.

01:28:01.190 --> 01:28:04.950
One's like, well, there's some
inventory building up there.

01:28:04.950 --> 01:28:06.980
So yes, that is really
important to do.

01:28:06.980 --> 01:28:09.530
And you should be able-- if
you have absolute variance,

01:28:09.530 --> 01:28:12.050
you might have created
a variance field,

01:28:12.050 --> 01:28:13.850
so you could potentially
use that and just

01:28:13.850 --> 01:28:16.230
calculate your bias
at the same time.

01:28:16.230 --> 01:28:17.180
Thank you so much.

01:28:17.180 --> 01:28:19.800
And thank you all
so much for joining.

01:28:19.800 --> 01:28:22.670
I just don't want anyone to feel
like you have to stay around,

01:28:22.670 --> 01:28:24.860
but if you want to hang
out with us all and maybe

01:28:24.860 --> 01:28:28.197
just chat informally,
feel free to stay on.

01:28:28.197 --> 01:28:30.530
We just want to say thank you
so much to Ralph and Laura

01:28:30.530 --> 01:28:33.760
for your awesome presentations.

